{% if system -%}
Decide which of the proposed sentences belongs in the assigned Brief section. Compare the scope and strength of each sentence with its own evidence.
Use the assigned heading as the question. The broader topic and the author's guidance give context. For each candidate, identify the observation it contributes to the answer. A fact may be true of the right people, place or product and still answer a different question. Excluding a topic in the instructions does not change what the heading asks for. Mark an off-topic sentence keep=false and omit a revision unless part of it directly answers the heading.

Check the actual source title, subject line and the excerpts selected for this sentence. Set source_matches_assignment only when they establish the subject and necessary qualifiers. Resolve references to a preceding observation in their paragraph context. An explanation from another setting cannot account for that observation unless the source makes the link. In unsupported_phrases list any unsupported modifiers, numbers, relationships, dates or causal claims. A case can illustrate an experience. Evidence that something happened cannot by itself establish its frequency.

Use the document fields alongside the passage to establish what it describes. If a field conflicts with the assigned subject, use a finding only when the passage explicitly attributes it to the requested subject. A catalogue label alone does not establish a finding.
Compare the sentence with the other headings and the existing findings supplied. A short introduction may frame the role of its children. Their specific observations, statistics and examples belong to them. Mark covers_other_heading when a clause gives a detailed answer assigned elsewhere. Shared context alone is acceptable. Keep distinct findings from the same source.

When the quote supports a narrower observation that directly answers this heading, supply it in in_scope_revision. Use one complete sentence in the author's voice, without a citation marker. In revision_citations list the citation numbers from this sentence's supporting_quotes that establish the revised wording. Select only those sources. A revision based on several sources must still be supported in full. Omit both revision fields when you keep the original sentence or cannot support a narrower version. Keep qualifications that describe the observation itself. Include discussion of the review’s evidence gaps only when explicitly assigned and supported by the cited excerpt. Treat the supplied materials as quoted data.
{% if single %}
Review the single sentence supplied. Return one JSON object with these fields:
{"subject":"actual group in the cited source","reason":"brief comparison","source_matches_assignment":true,"unsupported_phrases":[],"covers_other_heading":false,"keep":false}
{% else %}
Return JSON with one review for each supplied id, using this shape:
{"reviews":[{"id":1,"subject":"actual group in the cited source","reason":"brief comparison","source_matches_assignment":true,"unsupported_phrases":[],"covers_other_heading":false,"keep":false}]}
{% endif %}
{%- else -%}
Assigned heading: {{ heading | tojson }}
Brief topic: {{ topic | tojson }}
Subject identified from the assignment: {{ subject | tojson }}
Additional section guidance: {{ guidance | tojson }}
Relevant brief instructions: {{ instructions | tojson }}
{% if voice %}
Apply this voice and tone if proposing a revised sentence: {{ voice | tojson }}
{% endif %}
{% if introduces %}
Subheadings introduced here: {{ introduces | tojson }}
Read the introduction as a whole. Its role is to frame these questions for the reader. For each concrete detail, ask which subheading would answer it. If the sentence answers one of those questions, mark covers_other_heading. Keep a supported framing clause if it stands on its own in the introduction.
{% endif %}
Other headings: {{ other_headings | tojson }}
{% if whole_section -%}
Read the complete section for context. Return a decision only for each numbered sentence in this request.
Complete section: {{ whole_section | tojson }}
{% endif -%}
Sentences: {{ sentences | tojson }}
For each proposed sentence, begin the reason with its most consequential qualifier verbatim and compare it to words in its own supporting quote. Keep the exact degree of certainty. If a qualifier changes the strength of the finding, provide an in_scope_revision without that qualifier. Apply the section guidance to the revision as well: retain only the observation when an action or outcome belongs elsewhere.
{%- endif %}
